{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 16, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a medieval nobleman who reigned as the Count of Tripoli in the 12th century. He was born around 1165 and became the Count of Tripoli in 1187 after the death of his father Fulk V. Raymond III was a member of the powerful House of Lusignan and was married to Eschiva of Jerusalem.\n\n*Reign and Military Campaigns*\nRaymond III was a skilled military leader and expanded the County of Tripoli through a series of military campaigns. He captured the port city of Tyre from the Ayyubid dynasty in 1187 and incorporated it into the County of Tripoli. Raymond III also fought in the Third Crusade and participated in the Siege of Acre in 1191. He allied himself with King Richard I of England and other crusaders to retake the city of Acre from the Ayyubid forces.\n\n*Relationships and Family*\nRaymond III had several children with his wife Eschiva including Erard I and Raymond IV. Erard I succeeded his father as the Count of Tripoli and went on to have three sons of his own. Raymond IV would go on to become King of Cyprus and marry Isabella I of Jerusalem. Raymond III also had a son named William who became the Bishop of Sidon.\n\n*Succession and Death*\nRaymond III died in 1192 without a male heir. He was succeeded by his son Erard I who died shortly after taking the throne. Erard I was succeeded by his brother Raymond IV who went on to become King of Cyprus. Raymond III's legacy as a military leader and politician is still remembered today in the history of the County of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.001581966644152999}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001299296854995191}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.1985016018152237}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.194758028257638e-05}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.0002616301644593477}, {"token": "oli", "logp